
class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] numbers, int target) {
        int l = 0, r = numbers.length -1 ;
        int sum = 0;
        while( l < r ){
            sum = numbers[l] + numbers[r];

            if(sum < target ){
                l++;
            }
            if(sum > target){
                r--;

            }
            if(sum == target){
                int arr[] = {l+1, r+1};
                return arr;
            }

        }
     return null;
    }
}

/*
======================================================================
DESCRIPTION:
This code implements the Two-Pointer technique to find two distinct 
numbers in a sorted array that add up to a target value. By placing 
one pointer at the start (l) and one at the end (r), it dynamically 
adjusts their positions based on whether the current sum is too small 
or too large. It returns the 1-based indices of the two numbers once found.

COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS:
- Time Complexity: O(n)
  The algorithm scans the array at most once. In the worst-case scenario, 
  the two pointers will meet in the middle, meaning every element is 
  visited at most once. Thus, the time taken scales linearly with the 
  size of the array ($n$).

- Space Complexity: O(1)
  The algorithm only uses a few integer variables (l, r, sum) to track 
  the pointers and the calculation. No additional data structures are 
  created that scale with the input size, resulting in constant space.
======================================================================
